===L54A1===
A [[Drill purpose rifle|drill-purpose]] version of the various .303 Bren guns. It was mostly used by the [[Community Cadet Forces|Cadet Forces]] since these continued to use .303 Brens even after the Armed Forces proper had adopted the L4 Brens and the L7 GPMG.<ref>{{cite web |title=Centrefire automatic light machine gun - Bren L54A1 Drill Purpose (DP) - about 1945 |url=https://royalarmouries.org/collection/object/object-280965 |website=[[Royal Armouries]] |access-date=15 November 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Bren light machine gun |url=https://vickersmg.blog/in-use/accessories/associated-weapons/bren-light-machine-gun/ |website=Vickers MG Collection & Research Association |access-date=15 November 2025}}</ref>

==Besal gun==
{{main|Besal}}

The Besal or Faulkner light machine gun was a Bren-inspired emergency design developed in the aftermath of the French campaign and the Dunkirk Evacuation in which the [[British Expeditionary Force (World War II)|British Expeditionary Force]] lost almost 30,000 Brens and given the vulnerability of a single, well known, manufacturing site for the Bren at Royal Small Arms Factory Enfield. Developed in 1940 by Harry Faulker of the [[Birmingham Small Arms Company]], the Besal looks much like a Bren, but was developed with much simplified machining operations in mind to allow it to be produced in any machine shop. Unlike the Bren, the Besal was full automatic only rather than select fire and lacked a cocking handle, using the pistol grip instead. In Mark 2 format the Besal had no interchangeable parts with the Bren, but was designed to use Bren magazines.

===Taden gun===
{{main|Taden gun}}

The [[Taden gun]] was a post-war development of the Bren to use with the [[.280 British]] (7&nbsp;mm) intermediate round proposed to replace the .303 in British service. The Taden was belt-fed with either spade grips for MMG use or a buttstock and pistol grip for LMG use and would have replaced both the Bren and the Vickers machine gun. Although reliable{{citation needed|date=August 2012}} it was not accepted due to the US-driven standardization within NATO on the larger [[7.62×51mm NATO]] round.<ref>{{cite web |url-status=dead |url=http://www.quarry.nildram.co.uk/Assault.htm |title=Assault rifles and their ammunition |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140602021550/http://www.quarry.nildram.co.uk/Assault.htm |archive-date=June 2, 2014 |first1=Anthony G |last1=Williams |date=April 2014 }}</ref>

==X11==
The X11 was a belt-fed Bren derivative developed by RSAF Enfield after the cancellation of the EM-2 rifle and Taden machine gun, adapting the Taden concepts to a Bren derived weapon in the new [[7.62x51mm]] NATO standard round. It came second in trials behind the [[FN MAG]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=TAB |date=2017-09-17 |title=X11 Belt-Fed Bren Derivative |url=https://armourersbench.com/2017/09/17/x11-belt-fed-bren-derivative/ |access-date=2025-02-10 |website=The Armourers Bench |language=en}}</ref>

===Semiautomatic Bren guns===
Many nations' militaries have disposed of their Bren guns as surplus to their needs. Surplus Brens have been imported to the United States for sale to collectors, but due to US gun laws restricting the importation of automatic weapons such guns must be legally destroyed by cutting up the receivers. A number of US gunsmiths have manufactured new semiautomatic Brens by welding the pieces of destroyed receivers back together, with modifications to prevent the use of full automatic parts, and fitting new fire-control components capable of only semiautomatic fire. The balance of the parts are surplus Bren parts. Such "semiautomatic machine guns" are legally considered rifles under US Federal law and the laws of most states.{{Citation needed|date=January 2021}}

==Production==
===United Kingdom===
[[File:VeronicaFoster-RonnieBrenGunGirl-smoke.jpg|thumb|right|[[Veronica Foster]] as "Ronnie, the Bren Gun Girl", was a Canadian icon for women working in war production]]
Bren guns were produced at the [[Royal Small Arms Factory]], in [[Enfield, London]].{{Sfn|Hobart|1972|p=7}} The first Bren guns were built in September 1937, and by December, a total of 42 had been produced. Weekly production was 300 Brens a week in July 1938, and 400 a week in September 1939.{{Sfn|Hobart|1972|p=9}}
The Monotype Group{{efn|A consortium of Monotype, Daimler, Hercules Cycle, Climax Rock Drill, F. Tibbenham, British Fabricating Machine Co. and Sigmund Pumps}} produced Mark 2 Brens.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.iwm.org.uk/collections/item/object/30034907|title=Bren Mk 2|website=Imperial War Museums |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231105042058/https://www.iwm.org.uk/collections/item/object/30034907 |archive-date= Nov 5, 2023 }}</ref> Enfield produced a total of 220,000 Mark I Bren guns,{{Sfn|Dugelby|Stevens|1999|p=114}} 57,600 in Mark III,{{Sfn|Dugelby|Stevens|1999|p=171}} and 250 in Mark IV.{{Sfn|Dugelby|Stevens|1999|p=174}}

===Canada===
[[John Inglis and Company]] received a contract from the British and Canadian governments in March 1938 to supply 5,000 Bren machine guns to the UK and 7,000 Bren machine guns to Canada. Both countries shared the [[capital (economics)|capital]] costs of bringing in this new production facility.<ref>{{citation |url=https://archive.macleans.ca/article/1938/9/1/canadas-armament-mystery |title=Canada's Armament Mystery
|date=1 September 1938 |first= George A |last=Drew |work=Macleans |url-status=dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20220417040517/https://archive.macleans.ca/article/1938/9/1/canadas-armament-mystery |archive-date= Apr 17, 2022 }}</ref> Production started in 1940; by August 1942, the Inglis plant was averaging 10,000 Brens per month, and produced 186,000 Bren guns of all variants by the end of the war, including 43,000 chambered in [[7.92×57mm Mauser]] for export to the Chinese [[National Revolutionary Army]].{{Sfn|Grant|2013|p=22}}{{Sfn|Dugelby|Stevens|1999|pp=244–247}}

===India===
[[File:Keeping Watch.jpg|thumb|Indian soldier armed with domestically produced 7.62mm Bren (Gun Machine 7.62mm 1B), 2009.]]

In 1942, the [[Ishapore Arsenal]] began to produce Bren guns, and continued to do so long after the end of World War II, also manufacturing variants in [[7.62×51mm NATO]].{{Sfn|Grant|2013|p=22}}
A shadow factory for Ishapore was set up at [[Kanpur]] and produced .303 Brens before it was later rechambered to fire 7.62 NATO ammo in 1964<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://ofb.gov.in/unit/pages/SAF/history2|title=History |website=Small Arms Factory |publisher=Government of India |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200223141317/https://ofb.gov.in/unit/pages/SAF/history2|archive-date=2020-02-23}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://saf.gov.in/product.html |title=Product development history |website=Small Arms Factory |access-date=15 January 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190801055310/http://saf.gov.in/product.html |archive-date=1 August 2019 |url-status=dead}}</ref> as the 1A LMG.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.iwm.org.uk/collections/item/object/30029893|title=Gun Machine 7.62mm IA [Indian Bren gun]|website=Imperial War Museums}}</ref>

Prior to the end of manufacturing, the Bren was made in India as the 1B.

===Australia===
In 1940, the [[Lithgow Small Arms Factory]] in [[New South Wales]] began to manufacture Bren guns, producing a total of 17,249 by 1945.{{Sfn|Grant|2013|p=22}}

